WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.156
This is Play the Recorder from Zero, by FlautaQuest. The soprano recorder, one step at a time.

00:00:07.956 --> 00:00:10.588
Today: reading music and more notes.

00:00:12.388 --> 00:00:15.197
Reading music. Notes on the staff.

00:00:15.997 --> 00:00:25.735
You can play many tunes from letter names, but learning to read music opens up every songbook and every recorder method in the world. The basics are simpler than they look.

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:29.074
The staff and the treble clef.

00:00:29.874 --> 00:00:42.104
Music is written on a staff of five lines and four spaces. Recorder music uses the treble clef, the curly sign at the start. The higher a note sits on the staff, the higher it sounds.

00:00:42.904 --> 00:00:45.083
The notes you know sit here on the staff:

00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:53.698
G sits on the second line from the bottom. The treble clef curls around that line, and that is why it is also called the G clef.

00:00:54.498 --> 00:00:56.527
A sits in the space just above it.

00:00:57.327 --> 00:00:58.962
B sits on the middle line.

00:00:59.762 --> 00:01:13.632
Then, going up, C is in the next space, D on the fourth line, and so on. Going down from G, there is F in the space below, E on the bottom line, D just below the staff, and C on a short extra line below the staff.

00:01:15.132 --> 00:01:16.557
How long notes last.

00:01:17.357 --> 00:01:21.996
The shape of each note tells you how long to hold it. Counting in beats:

00:01:22.796 --> 00:01:26.068
A whole note, an open oval, lasts four beats.

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:30.867
A half note, an open oval with a stem, lasts two beats.

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:35.623
A quarter note, a filled oval with a stem, lasts one beat.

00:01:36.423 --> 00:01:41.111
An eighth note, with a flag or joined to another by a beam, lasts half a beat.

00:01:41.911 --> 00:01:48.729
Each of them has a matching rest, a sign that means silence for the same time. Rests are good places to breathe.

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:52.050
Bars and time signatures.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:02:05.424
Vertical lines divide the music into bars. The two numbers at the start, the time signature, tell you how many beats there are in each bar. In 4/4 there are four; in 3/4 there are three.

00:02:06.924 --> 00:02:08.247
Remember the octave.

00:02:09.047 --> 00:02:22.474
As chapter 1 explains, soprano recorder music is written an octave lower than it sounds. When you play the G on the second line, the recorder sounds the G one octave higher. Just read and play the note as written.

00:02:23.274 --> 00:02:30.078
Tip: Say the note names out loud before you play a new piece, and clap the rhythm. Your fingers will thank you.

00:02:31.578 --> 00:02:32.310
Practice.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:37.910
First: draw a staff with a treble clef, and write G, A and B on it.

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:44.959
Second: find a simple song in a book or online, written in standard notation, and name its notes.

00:02:45.759 --> 00:02:50.093
Third: clap the rhythm of Hot Cross Buns while counting the beats out loud.

00:02:51.893 --> 00:02:54.548
More notes. Up and down from G.

00:02:55.348 --> 00:03:08.083
With B, A and G you have the middle of the recorder. Now you can add notes above and below. In the ebook, the high C, D and E are written C’, D’ and E’ to tell them apart from the low ones.

00:03:09.583 --> 00:03:11.060
High C and high D.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.245
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:03:15.045 --> 00:03:20.165
C’: thumb and second finger. Watch out: the first finger is lifted.

00:03:20.965 --> 00:03:24.874
D’: only the second finger. The thumb hole is open.

00:03:26.374 --> 00:03:28.141
Low notes with the right hand.

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:32.114
Going down from G, the right hand starts to work:

00:03:32.914 --> 00:03:35.299
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:03:36.099 --> 00:03:48.109
The lower the note, the more fingers, and the softer the breath. The low C in particular needs very gentle air and every hole perfectly sealed. Do not worry if it takes a few weeks to sound well.

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:58.594
On most modern baroque recorders, holes 6 and 7 are double holes: two small holes side by side. For these notes, cover both halves completely.

00:04:00.094 --> 00:04:01.152
F and F sharp.

00:04:01.952 --> 00:04:07.674
On a baroque recorder, F uses a forked fingering: one hole open with closed holes below it.

00:04:08.474 --> 00:04:10.859
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:12.359 --> 00:04:14.274
Pinching the thumb: high E.

00:04:15.074 --> 00:04:28.059
To reach the notes above high D, you partly open the thumb hole, leaving a small gap at the top of it. This is called pinching. Bend the thumb a little so that the nail-side tip covers most of the hole, not all of it.

00:04:28.859 --> 00:04:31.244
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:32.044 --> 00:04:37.724
High notes need a little more, and faster, air than low ones, but still much less than you might think.

00:04:39.224 --> 00:04:42.226
A tune with high D: Mary Had a Little Lamb.

00:04:43.026 --> 00:04:45.411
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:46.211 --> 00:04:54.707
Tip: Learn one new note at a time. Play it as a long note, then move between it and a note you already know, until the change is smooth.

00:04:56.207 --> 00:04:56.939
Practice.

00:04:57.739 --> 00:05:04.512
First: play G A B C’ D’, then back down. Go slowly, and watch the first finger on C’.

00:05:05.312 --> 00:05:13.015
Second: play G, then E, then D, then C, with long, soft notes. Listen for any leaks.

00:05:13.815 --> 00:05:18.203
Third: play the forked F between G and E: G F E F G.

00:05:19.003 --> 00:05:21.575
Fourth: learn Mary Had a Little Lamb.

00:05:23.375 --> 00:05:24.624
That's all for today.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:26.815
If you want to keep going:

00:05:27.615 --> 00:05:38.435
FlautaQuest is the app behind the ebook. It teaches the soprano recorder from zero, in short, step-by-step lessons, in the style of language-learning apps like Duolingo.

00:05:39.235 --> 00:05:42.141
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to go with it:

00:05:42.941 --> 00:05:45.176
holding the recorder and breathing well.

00:05:45.976 --> 00:05:47.866
the fingerings of each new note.

00:05:48.666 --> 00:05:50.253
reading simple music.

00:05:51.053 --> 00:05:54.000
learning your first songs, one step at a time.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.882
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who wants to start playing the recorder.

00:06:00.682 --> 00:06:05.517
This episode of Play the Recorder from Zero was narrated with a synthetic voice.

